course: dictionary
lesson: 14706
title: Likely but not certain: probable
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Probable means likely to happen or be true, but not certain. When you read «It is probable that it will rain this afternoon», the writer is making a sensible guess from clues, like dark grey clouds. They do not know for sure.
explain: Look at the words around it to check. «The probable cause of the fire was a dropped candle.» Cause means the reason something happened, so probable tells you the most likely reason, based on evidence. Ask yourself: is the writer sure, or making a good guess?
explain: Probable sits between two other words. Certain means it definitely will happen, and impossible means it cannot happen. Probable is strong, but it is not certain. «It is highly probable that she will win» means she is very likely to win, not that she has already won.
explain: In maths you may meet probable on a chance scale: impossible, unlikely, even chance, probable, certain. Try the swap test: put likely into the sentence. If it still makes sense, probable is the right meaning.
fact: Probable means likely to happen or be true, but not certain.
fact: «Probable» is an adjective. «It is probable that the match will be cancelled.»
fact: In informal talk, a probable is someone likely to be chosen, for example for a team.
fact: Probable is a strong word, but it is not certain: certain means definitely, and impossible means cannot happen.
fact: In science and stories, «the probable cause» means the most likely reason, based on evidence.
exercise: «It is probable that the match will be cancelled because of the rain.» What does probable mean here? | Impossible | Definitely, with no doubt at all | *Likely, but not certain | Happy and excited
exercise: «The probable cause of the broken window was a football.» What is the probable cause? | A kind of window glass | *The most likely reason | The colour of the football | The one thing that is certain
exercise: Ruby says probable means the same as certain. Is she right? | Yes, but only in maths lessons | *No, probable means likely, but not certain | Yes, the two words mean exactly the same | No, probable means impossible
test: «Jake is a probable choice for the team, so he will probably be picked.» What does probable choice suggest? | A kind of team shirt | A player who has already been picked | A player who will never play | *Someone likely to be picked
test: Which word is closest in meaning to probable? | *Likely | Impossible | Certain | Unknown
exam: Which sentence uses probable correctly? | Two plus two is probable to equal five | The number seven is probable to be purple | The sun is probable to rise in the north tomorrow | *It is probable that the bus will be late, because the road is flooded
